      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, let's reframe the CI/CD pipeline stages with a focus on the technical aspects and quality gates.\n\n**Code Commit:** At this initial stage, the code is committed to the version control system. The quality gate here should ensure that the code adheres to the project's coding standards and passes any pre-commit hooks that are in place. This gate acts as a first line of defense against introducing errors into the codebase.\n\n**Automated Testing:** Once the code is committed, it triggers automated tests. The quality gate at this stage should verify that all unit tests pass, and if applicable, integration and end-to-end tests are successful. This ensures that the new code does not break existing functionality and that it integrates well with the rest of the system.\n\n**Staging:** The code is then deployed to a staging environment that closely mirrors production. The quality gate here should include smoke tests to verify that the most critical functions of the application are working as expected. Additionally, performance tests should be conducted to ensure that the application meets the required performance benchmarks.\n\n**Production:** Finally, the code is deployed to the production environment. The quality gate at this stage should include comprehensive monitoring to detect any issues that may arise post-deployment. This includes error tracking,",
